,"Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","en:Egyptian deities"],"links":[["Egyptian","Egyptian"],["mythology","mythology"],["Egypt","Egypt"],["god","god"],["jackal","jackal"]],"raw_glosses":["(Egyptian mythology) In the mythology of ancient Egypt, the god of the dead and tombs, commonly depicted with the head of a jackal."],"glosses":["In the mythology of ancient Egypt, the god of the dead and tombs, commonly depicted with the head of a jackal."],"tags":["Egyptian"],"id":"0acbe361-5d07-41b9-824a-07cfaf3bd893"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"anuria":{"id":"996986ce-f373-4e57-a7b1-147e42c6b810","word":"anuria","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"a condition in which the kidneys do not produce urine","tags":["feminine"],"word":"anuria"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Clinically defined as urine output of less than 100 ml in 24 hours, anuria indicates either urinary tract obstruction or acute renal failure due to various mechanisms.[…]Anuria is rare; even with renal failure, the kidneys usually produce at least 75 ml of urine daily.","ref":"2007, Nain D. Chohan, editor, Nursing: Interpreting Signs & Symptoms, page 39:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[68,74],[170,176]],"id":"215f61c1-8a52-47a4-9d15-da1b666cfe07"},{"text":"Changes in urinary pattern such as polyuria, oliguria, anuria, enuresis, and excessive thirst can be associated with tubular dysfunction.","ref":"2008, Ronald M. Perkin, Dale A. Newton, James D. Swift, editors, Pediatric Hospital Medicine: Textbook of Inpatient Management, 2nd edition, page 359:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[55,61]],"id":"73c27364-e24a-4ba4-a369-6b88c3a3d246"},{"text":"It goes without saying, therefore, that obstructive anuria can occur only if the outflow from both kidneys or from the only functioning kidney is obstructed.","ref":"2011, Boris Lams, “Oliguria”, in Mark Kinirons, Harold Ellis, editors, French's Index of Differential Diagnosis: An A-Z, page 467:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[52,58]],"id":"4d5d0501-7aa4-444a-bbc5-55b2221a65a8"}],"topics":["medicine","sciences"],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms prefixed with an-","English terms suffixed with -uria","English terms with quotations","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 6 entries","Pages with entries","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Indonesian translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","en:Medicine"],"links":[["medicine","medicine"],["kidney","kidney"],["urine","urine"]],"raw_glosses":["(medicine) A condition in which the kidneys do not produce urine."],"glosses":["A condition in which the kidneys do not produce urine."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"fa90cc40-b40c-4dae-b478-f92e1dfe4508"}],"related":[{"word":"anuresis"},{"word":"anuretic"},{"word":"anuric"},{"word":"enuresis"},{"word":"oligoanuria"},{"word":"oliguria"},{"word":"polyuria"}],"synonyms":[]},"anuresis":{"id":"c9cdd806-77aa-4fd5-8071-0435ca6b865a","word":"anuresis","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"the inability to urinate","tags":["feminine"],"word":"anurese"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Kaido [35] describes a 53-year-old Japanese lady whose index presentation was with vomiting, anuresis, and clouded conscious state on a background of 6 months of apathy.","ref":"2010, Perminder S. Sachdev, Matcheri S. Keshavan, editors, Secondary Schizophrenia, Cambridge University Press, page 235:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[93,101]],"id":"51570dc6-0b1c-4155-8100-acf5a0912022"},{"text":"Suffice it to say that the urge to look over/down at their penises is powerful and the motives behind this urge so complex as to cause anuresis (which in turn ups the trauma).","ref":"2012, David Foster Wallace, Consider The Lobster: Essays and Arguments, page vii:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[135,143]],"id":"2eb9ec6c-ea6a-4831-a6a5-104d38184108"},{"text":"2012 March, Paul Nikolaidis, Nancy A. Hammond (guest editors), Frank H. Miller (consulting editor), Genitourinary Imaging, Radiologic Clinics of North America, Volume 50, Number 2, unnumbered page,\nAlthough rare, symptoms may include lumbar or hip pain, dysuria, oliguria, anuresis, and, more rarely, hematuria and pollakiuria.","id":"cd3dbbb4-4f47-4389-8b7a-994408af2a90"}],"topics":["medicine","sciences"],"categories":["English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms prefixed with an-","English terms suffixed with -uresis","English terms with quotations","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 2 entries","Pages with entries","Quotation templates to be cleaned","Rhymes:English/iːsɪs","Rhymes:English/iːsɪs/3 syllables","Terms with Indonesian translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","en:Medicine"],"links":[["medicine","medicine"],["inability","inability"],["urinate","urinate"],["retention","retention"],["urine","urine"],["bladder","bladder"]],"raw_glosses":["(medicine) The inability to urinate; the retention of urine in the bladder."],"glosses":["The inability to urinate; the retention of urine in the bladder."],"tags":["uncountable"],"id":"95728688-e945-434f-b50d-97294bb1f6e1"}],"related":[{"word":"anuretic"},{"word":"anuria"},{"word":"enuresis"},{"word":"dysuria"},{"word":"haematuria"},{"word":"hematuria"},{"word":"oliguria"},{"word":"pollakiuria"},{"word":"polyuria"}],"synonyms":[]},"a number of":{"id":"59d7f334-4c96-4da0-8791-759c8fc6744a","word":"a number of","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"several","word":"um número de"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"several","word":"uma quantidade de"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"several","word":"um tanto de"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"several of","word":"um tanto de"}],"pos":"phrase","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"A number of people have commented on it.","bold_text_offsets":[[0,11]],"type":"example","id":"de7127c4-c72e-4e7d-84bc-eba1a240ca21"},{"text":"a goodly number of cheeses","bold_text_offsets":[[0,1],[9,18]],"type":"example","id":"cd050ccb-68fe-4b95-9fe5-911b6af60c7d"},{"text":"There are a large number of problems with the log-in system.","bold_text_offsets":[[6,7],[10,11],[13,14],[18,27]],"type":"example","id":"5d64d3bb-377c-4436-b851-e4643fcf5c5f"}],"links":[["Several","several"]],"glosses":["Several."],"categories":["English terms with usage examples"],"id":"fd5d1201-31a8-4f46-a15b-4ee6cebc21dc"},{"examples":[{"text":"I spoke with a number of them about it.","bold_text_offsets":[[13,24]],"type":"example","id":"e58ed2fe-40ac-4bfc-a650-4bd9733b2e09"}],"glosses":["Several of."],"categories":["English terms with usage examples"],"id":"800cc908-000f-4aa5-a57e-54b54a07770a"},{"links":[["a","a#English"],["number","number#English"],["of","of#English"]],"glosses":["Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see a, number, of."],"id":"951b209d-6703-4e83-bd8c-38aad46ecef4"}],"related":[{"word":"any number of"}],"synonyms":[]},"anuran":{"id":"918d7476-54fe-4274-baab-1a615948c0fd","word":"anuran","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"of or relating to the order Anura","word":"anuro"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"As a further illustration, we may take the results of experiments in which a Urodele organiser (from Triton alpestris) is grafted into an Anuran embryo (Bufo vulgaris).","ref":"1934, Julian S[orell] Huxley, G[avin] R[ylands] de Beer, “Organisers: Inducers of Differentiation”, in The Elements of Experimental Embryology, Cambridge: At the University Press, →OCLC, page 142:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[138,144]],"id":"8fb690cd-de25-4b7c-9283-1a4f6edf71e9"},{"text":"It is well known that an anuran tadpole or an insect larva will never turn into its adult form if the respective endogenous metamorphic hormones, thyroxine and ecdysone, were withdrawn or prevented from reaching the target tissues.","ref":"1971, J. R. Tata, “Protein Synthesis during Amphibian Metamorphosis”, in A[ron] A[rthur] Moscona, Alberto Monroy, editors, Current Topics in Developmental Biology, volume 6, New York, N.Y.; London: Academic Press, →ISBN, page 79:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[25,31]],"id":"651c2726-0c75-4698-96b8-a68fa9c43ff8"},{"text":"[I]njection of urodele DNA into an anuran (frog) egg produces an extra \"balancer\" (i.e., protrusion on the chin of the embryo which may or may not be a real balancer), but so does injection of the DNA of this anuran into its own egg.","ref":"1989, Eric H[arris] Davidson, “An Analysis of Niu Mengchang’s Research on Transformation by RNA”, in Dean H. Hamer, Shain-dow Kung, editors, Biotechnology in China, Washington, D.C.: National Academy Press, →ISBN, page 94:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[35,41],[209,215]],"id":"2f4d1b00-a73a-459f-ae9d-90f470b361a8"}],"links":[["Anura","Anura"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"raw_glosses":["(batrachology) Of or relating to the order Anura."],"glosses":["Of or relating to the order Anura."],"tags":["not-comparable"],"id":"f7f87265-7796-4b76-8b66-fc6f7951e373"}],"related":[{"word":"froggish"},{"word":"froggy"},{"word":"froglike"},{"word":"frogly"},{"word":"ranarian"},{"word":"ranine"},{"word":"toadish"},{"word":"toadlike"},{"word":"toadly"}],"synonyms":[{"word":"batrachian"}]},"anusvara":{"id":"90e13273-190f-4d0e-a617-3ae05df6005a","word":"anusvara","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt",